{
  "title": "📐 Basic Proportionality Theorem — Lines Parallel to a Side",
  "hook": "Imagine slicing a triangle with a line parallel to its base. The two pieces you cut off have a magical property: the sides are divided in exactly the same ratio! This is the Basic Proportionality Theorem (BPT).",
  "explanation": "<div style=\"background:#F3E5F5;border-left:4px solid #7B1FA2;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0\"><strong style=\"color:#7B1FA2\">BPT (Thales' Theorem):</strong> If a line is drawn parallel to one side of a triangle, it divides the other two sides <b>in the same ratio</b>.</div><div style=\"text-align:center;margin:10px 0\"><svg width=\"260\" height=\"200\"><polygon points=\"130,20 30,180 230,180\" fill=\"#E8F5E9\" stroke=\"#2E7D32\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"65\" y1=\"120\" x2=\"195\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#E65100\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6\"/><text x=\"125\" y=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"195\" font-weight=\"bold\">B</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"195\" font-weight=\"bold\">C</text><text x=\"55\" y=\"115\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#E65100\">D</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"115\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#E65100\">E</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"135\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#E65100\">DE ∥ BC</text></svg></div><div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E3F2FD,#BBDEFB);border-left:4px solid #1565C0;border-radius:8px;padding:12px 16px;margin:12px 0;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;font-size:1.05rem;text-align:center\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;text-transform:uppercase;font-weight:700;color:#1565C0;margin-bottom:4px\">BPT</div><strong>DE ∥ BC  ⟹  AD/DB = AE/EC</strong></div><p><b>Converse:</b> If AD/DB = AE/EC, then DE ∥ BC.</p><div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> BPT also means AD/AB = AE/AC = DE/BC — the line divides all three ratios proportionally.</div>",
  "worked_example": "<b>Example:</b> In △ABC, DE ∥ BC with AD = 4, DB = 6, AE = 3. Find EC.<br><br><div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div><div><strong>Apply BPT</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">AD/DB = AE/EC</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div><div><strong>Substitute</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">4/6 = 3/EC</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div><div><strong>Cross-multiply</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">4 × EC = 6 × 3 = 18</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\"><div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#4A90D9;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">4</div><div><strong>Solve</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">EC = 18/4 = 4.5 cm</span></div></div></div>",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "In △ABC, D is on AB and E is on AC. AD = 5, AB = 12, AE = 10. Find AC if DE ∥ BC.",
    "hint": "AD/AB = AE/AC. So 5/12 = 10/AC.",
    "answer": "AC = 10 × 12/5 = 24."
  },
  "fun_fact": "Thales of Miletus (624-546 BC) is said to have measured the height of the Great Pyramid using this very principle — by comparing the shadow of the pyramid with the shadow of a stick of known height!"
}